---
id: PY-009
type: rule
family: python-reliability
title: os.system / shell=True / pickle.loads / yaml.load
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 9

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.py]
allowlist: ['**/test_*.py', '**/*_test.py', '**/tests/**', '**/conftest.py', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\b(os\.system|os\.popen)\s*\(|\bshell\s*=\s*True\b|\bpickle\.loads?\s*\(|\byaml\.load\s*\('

related: [PY-008, AP-008]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

These Python surfaces turn a string (or a byte stream) into process or object
execution. Prefer:

- `subprocess.run([...], check=True)` with an argument list (no shell)
- `yaml.safe_load` (preferred) rather than `yaml.load`
- a typed serializer (pydantic, msgspec) instead of `pickle` for untrusted input

Detection is lexical: any `yaml.load(` call is flagged, including calls that
pass a safe loader on the same line — the regex tier does not distinguish
loader arguments. Prefer `yaml.safe_load` and suppress with a reason only when
a reviewed explicit-loader form is intentional.

`shell=True` is flagged even when the command is a static list today, because
the flag is the hazard — it reopens command injection as soon as the command is
built by concatenation. Suppress with a reason if the call is genuinely
hard-coded and reviewed.
